# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the Next.js project (App Router).

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (version 1.6.1) using npm
- Updated `package.json` with the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` to reflect the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Root Layout Update (`src/app/layout.tsx`)
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag, after the `<Providers>{children}</Providers>` component
- This placement ensures analytics tracking is initialized for all pages in the application

## Implementation Details

**Project Type:** Next.js App Router (uses `app` directory)

**Package Manager:** npm (detected via `package-lock.json`)

**Root Layout Location:** `web/src/app/layout.tsx`

The Analytics component was strategically placed after the children content to ensure:
1. Core application content loads first
2. Analytics doesn't block critical rendering
3. Tracking is active across all routes and pages

## Verification Steps Completed

✅ Successfully installed @vercel/analytics package via npm  
✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)  
✅ TypeScript compilation passed with no errors  
✅ Linter ran without introducing new errors (pre-existing warnings/errors remain unchanged)  
✅ All changes staged in git (package.json, package-lock.json, src/app/layout.tsx)

## Notes

- The Analytics component will automatically track page views and web vitals
- No additional configuration is needed for basic analytics functionality
- Analytics data will be visible in the Vercel dashboard once deployed
- The implementation follows Vercel's official documentation for App Router projects
